          "question": "Which conditions covered here have a three-character code that takes no further character?",
          "answer": "**Only one example in the material I searched is confirmed as complete at three characters: I10, essential (primary) hypertension.** The coding guidance says I10 \"takes none\" of the extra characters, unlike most ICD-10-CM categories, which need a fourth, fifth or sixth character (*Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family*).\n\nThe other conditions I checked all need more characters:\n- **Type 2 diabetes (E11)** is never used at three characters. The fourth and later characters name the complication (*Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories*).\n- **COPD (J44)** uses its fourth character to show whether a complication is present (*Coding chronic obstructive pulmonary disease: J44 and its two complication codes*).\n\nI could not check the other categories you may have in mind, such as those in the endocrine chapter (E15 to E89). The retrieved passages list their block ranges but do not say whether each one subdivides, so I cannot say which are complete at three characters.\n\nTo answer for your specific conditions, please name them or paste the list. I will check each one against the coding passages.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
